  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several astrophysical transients that can produce high-energy emission, showing how compact objects, jets, surrounding matter, cosmic rays, neutrinos, gamma rays, and sometimes gravitational waves are linked. Across the panels, a common theme is a central explosion or merger launching a narrow bipolar jet, with particles and radiation escaping either along the jet axis or through surrounding ejecta, winds, disks, or gas.\n\nThe examples include a neutron star merger associated with a short gamma-ray burst, a collapsar-like long gamma-ray burst inside a progenitor star, a tidal disruption event where a star is torn apart by a massive compact object, a blazar flare from an accreting black hole with a jet and dusty environment, and an engine-driven supernova with a choked jet buried inside expanding material. It also shows an ordinary supernova embedded in circumstellar matter, and a double black hole merger occurring in gas, followed by a possible jet-producing remnant environment.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes that different source classes may generate cosmic rays and neutrinos under different geometries and levels of obscuration, while only some are clearly accompanied by gravitational-wave signals.",
